import type { Lesson } from "./lessons";

export const lesson018 = {
  number: 18,
  module: "Module 1: How a Chip Is Physically Made",
  title: "Masks, Reticles and the Reticle Limit — The Master Patterns Behind Every Lithography Step",
  date: "2026-08-24",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 013: CMP repeatedly flattens wafer topography so later layers can still be patterned accurately.

Lesson 014: CMP consumables show how wafer volume can create recurring materials revenue around an installed equipment base.

Lesson 015: Fab output is governed by a network of queues, yield risks and migrating bottlenecks rather than one universally dominant tool.

Lesson 016: Wafer-start capacity becomes useful only after translating it through utilization, die size, yield and downstream constraints.

Lesson 017: Large dies suffer both fewer candidates per wafer and greater defect exposure, pushing AI designs toward chiplets when packaging economics allow it.

The big idea

Lithography does not invent a circuit pattern while the wafer is inside the scanner.

It copies a master pattern onto the wafer.

That master pattern is carried on a photomask, often called a reticle in advanced wafer lithography.

The simple mental model is:

designer's layout → mask/reticle → scanner projects pattern → photoresist records it → etch or another process transfers it into the wafer

A modern chip needs many patterned layers, so it needs many mask patterns. This creates two important economics: mask sets are expensive and slow to change, and the scanner's maximum exposure field helps set the practical size limit of one conventional monolithic die.

1. What is physically on a mask?

Think of a mask as an extraordinarily precise stencil.

It is typically a flat transparent substrate with a carefully manufactured pattern that controls how lithography light reaches the wafer. EUV masks are physically different because EUV optics are reflective rather than transmissive, but the role is similar: encode the pattern that the scanner must reproduce.

The scanner does not normally print the mask at full size. Projection optics reduce the mask pattern onto the wafer, commonly by 4× in leading lithography systems.

So if a feature on the mask is 40 nm wide, the projected wafer-scale feature would be roughly 10 nm before accounting for the much more complicated realities of imaging and process correction.

2. Why one chip needs many masks

A finished processor is not patterned in one exposure.

Different layers define different structures: transistor features, contacts, metal wiring and many other elements. Each lithographically distinct layer needs the appropriate mask pattern.

That means an advanced process can require a large mask set rather than one master stencil.

The key economic consequence is that design changes late in development can be expensive. If engineers change a layer, the relevant mask may need to be remade and requalified before wafers can run the revised design.

This is why a "tape-out" matters. It is the handoff point where the design database is prepared for manufacturing. After tape-out, changing the physical design becomes much more costly than editing software.

3. Masks themselves must be nearly perfect

A defect on a wafer may kill one die.

A repeating defect on a mask is more dangerous because the scanner can reproduce that defect across many dies and many wafers.

So mask manufacturing has its own precision ecosystem: writing the pattern, inspecting it, repairing defects when possible, measuring critical dimensions and protecting the finished mask from contamination.

This is a useful systems insight: lithography yield depends not only on the scanner. The mask, resist, optics, computational corrections, wafer positioning and process control all have to work together.

4. Why masks are economically sticky

Leading-edge mask sets can cost millions of dollars, with exact costs varying substantially by node, design and how many advanced layers are involved.

The important scale intuition is not one exact price. It is that a mask set is expensive enough to matter in chip-development economics but tiny relative to the revenue of a successful high-volume AI processor.

That creates a fixed-cost effect.

If a mask set costs several million dollars and supports only 1,000 sellable chips, mask cost per chip is enormous.

If it supports millions of chips, the mask cost per chip becomes small.

So advanced-node economics strongly reward volume. The huge up-front engineering and mask costs can be amortized across enormous unit or revenue pools.

5. The reticle limit

Now connect this to Lesson 017.

A lithography scanner can expose only a finite rectangular field in one conventional shot. For mainstream advanced 4× systems, the maximum wafer exposure field is roughly 26 mm × 33 mm, or about 858 mm².

That does not mean every 858 mm² design is practical, nor that every product uses the entire field. But it gives you the right order of magnitude for the maximum conventional monolithic die footprint.

This is the reticle limit.

It is why an AI chip cannot simply keep becoming one infinitely large piece of silicon.

Once designers approach this physical field boundary, more compute must increasingly come from architectural changes: multiple dies, chiplets, multiple packages or more systems connected together.

6. Why the limit matters economically

Suppose compute demand wants 2× more transistors.

If the current die is already near the reticle limit, the designer cannot simply double die area.

It has to find another path:

- use denser transistors on a newer node;
- improve architecture;
- split the design across multiple dies;
- or scale across multiple packaged accelerators.

Each solution shifts the bottleneck somewhere else.

Moving to a new node increases dependence on leading-edge foundry capacity and yield.

Splitting into chiplets increases advanced-packaging and die-to-die interconnect requirements.

Scaling across packages increases networking, power and cooling requirements.

This is a core supply-chain principle: physical limits at one layer create demand in adjacent layers.

7. Reticle size versus wafer size

Do not confuse the 300 mm wafer with the reticle field.

The wafer is the large circular manufacturing surface.

The reticle field is the much smaller rectangular area the scanner patterns at one location before stepping or scanning to the next location.

The scanner repeats the field across the wafer.

So one wafer may contain many copies of a large accelerator die, but each individual conventional die must fit inside the scanner's printable field.

This is why yesterday's die-per-wafer math and today's reticle limit are related but different constraints.

Wafer geometry limits how many dies fit.

Reticle geometry limits how large one conventional die can be.

8. Can the industry print beyond one reticle?

Yes, with techniques such as stitching, where patterns from multiple exposure fields are joined to create a structure larger than one normal field.

But stitching adds alignment and manufacturing complexity, so it is not a free escape from the limit.

Large interposers used in advanced packaging are an important place where beyond-reticle techniques can matter. That is a preview of Topic 3: once multiple compute dies must communicate at very high bandwidth, the package itself starts looking like a sophisticated miniature system.

So the reticle limit is not merely a lithography fact. It is one reason advanced packaging became strategically important to AI.

9. The supplier map

ASML supplies the advanced lithography scanners, but it does not own the entire mask ecosystem.

Mask blanks, mask writing, inspection, metrology, resist and computational lithography involve additional specialized suppliers and internal capabilities at chipmakers and foundries.

KLA participates in mask inspection and metrology. Applied Materials has mask-related inspection and process equipment. Photronics, Toppan and DNP are notable commercial photomask manufacturers, although the exact supplier structure differs by technology and customer.

The investment lesson is the same one we have used throughout the course: map the exact physical choke point before assigning value to a company merely because it touches the process.

10. The analyst translation ladder

When you hear that an AI chip is "reticle-sized," translate the phrase:

1. The die is approaching the maximum conventional lithography exposure field.

2. Gross dies per wafer will be relatively low because the die is physically large.

3. Yield becomes especially important because each die exposes a large area to defects.

4. Further scaling cannot rely mainly on making one die larger.

5. More compute therefore pushes toward denser nodes, chiplets, advanced packaging and system-level scaling.

That is how one lithography constraint propagates through the AI supply chain.

Remember these 6 things

1. A mask or reticle carries the master pattern that lithography reproduces on the wafer.

2. Advanced chips need many mask patterns because fabrication builds many separately patterned layers.

3. Mask defects are dangerous because they can repeat across many dies and wafers.

4. Leading-edge mask sets are expensive fixed development costs, so high production volume improves their per-chip economics.

5. A conventional advanced lithography field is roughly 26 × 33 mm on the wafer, giving an order-of-magnitude reticle area near 858 mm².

6. The reticle limit helps push AI scaling from giant monolithic dies toward chiplets, advanced packaging and system-level interconnect.

Quick check

Why does the reticle limit create opportunity for advanced packaging?

Because once a single conventional die cannot grow much larger, designers can combine multiple smaller dies into one package. That relieves the monolithic die-size constraint but increases the need for high-bandwidth die-to-die connections, interposers and precise multi-die assembly.

